['Here is an example implementation in C# using async/await for efficient data retrieval and transformation with potential concurrency issues handled by locks. Please note that this is a simplified version and actual implementation may require more considerations like error handling, retry logic, transaction management etc.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class ProcessResult
{
    public int ItemsProcessed { get; set; }
    public List<Exception> Errors { get; set; } = new List<Exception>();
}

public async Task<ProcessResult> ProcessDataAsync(List<string> inputData, SqlConnection connection, Func<string, string> transformationRules)
{
    var result = new ProcessResult();
    object lockObject = new object();

    List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();

    foreach (var item in inputData)
    {
        tasks.Add(Task.Run(async () =>
        {
            try
            {
                var transformedData = transformationRules(item); // Perform data transformation asynchronously based on each item.

                await connection.OpenAsync(); 
                using (var command = new SqlCommand("INSERT INTO TableName VALUES(@value)", connection))
                {
                    command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@value", transformedData);

                    lock(lockObject) // Using a lock to prevent concurrent database access.
                    {
                        await command.ExecuteNonQueryAsync();
                        ++result.ItemsProcessed;
                    }
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                result.Errors.Add(ex);
            }
        }));
    }

    await Task.WhenAll(tasks); // Await all tasks to ensure that they are complete before returning the results.

    return result;
}
```
']

['Here's an example solution to your task written in C# using LINQ to XML. It uses XDocument class to handle the XML file and Dictionaries to store parsed values. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Xml.Linq;

public class GameSettings
{
    private const string SettingsFilePath = "settings.xml";
    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> settings = new();

    public void Load()
    {
        XDocument doc = XDocument.Load(SettingsFilePath);

        foreach (XElement element in doc.Root.Elements())
            settings[element.Name.LocalName] = element.Value;
    }

    public int GetIntSetting(string settingName, int defaultValue)
    {
        return int.TryParse(settings[settingName], out var value) ? value : defaultValue;
    }

    public void SetIntSetting(string settingName, int value)
    {
        settings[settingName] = value.ToString();
    }

    public void Save()
    {
        XDocument doc = new XDocument(new XElement("Settings"));

        foreach (var kvp in settings)
            doc.Root.Add(new XElement(kvp.Key, kvp.Value));

        doc.Save(SettingsFilePath);
    }
}
```

This class reads and writes XML files to a dictionary for efficient lookup. It gracefully handles missing or invalid settings by providing defaults and saving back to the XML file. 

In terms of loading performance, LINQ to XML is designed to be memory-efficient and fast for large documents. However, this approach might not scale well if your configuration files are extremely large or there's a need to handle multiple simultaneous changes, in which case you may want to consider a different approach that can more efficiently update the file without loading everything into memory at once.

Error handling is straightforward and falls back to default values when encountering invalid settings. This makes it easy to diagnose issues by inspecting the XML file, while providing a safe fallback in case of errors. The trade-off here is that configuration updates become more complex due to having to manually update the XML file.

In terms of data storage, this approach uses an in-memory dictionary for quick lookups, but there's also the option to store everything in XML and load only what's needed when needed, which can be more memory-efficient if your configuration files are large. The trade-off here is increased complexity due to having to parse XML on every request.
']

['Here's a basic example in ASP.NET Core Web API with Entity Framework and some logging using Serilog. Here I'm assuming your User class is something like this:

```CSharp
public class User {
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
    public DateTime BirthDate { get; set; }
