["Here is a simple implementation of such a function in JavaScript using `Buffer` object for data handling and concurrency via `Atomics` API for thread safety:

```javascript
function copyBytes(chunks, offset, length) {
    // Check if parameters are valid
    if (!Array.isArray(chunks)) throw new Error('Invalid chunks parameter');
    if (typeof offset !== 'number' || offset < 0) throw new Error('Invalid offset parameter');
    if (typeof length !== 'number' || length < 0) throw new Error('Invalid length parameter');

    // Create a buffer of the required size
    let result = Buffer.alloc(length);

    // Track how much data we have copied and from which chunk
    let bytesCopied = 0;
    let currentChunkIndex = 0;

    while (bytesCopied < length) {
        const currentChunk = chunks[currentChunkIndex];

        // Check if the current chunk is a valid Buffer instance and has any data left
        if (!Buffer.isBuffer(currentChunk) || offset >= currentChunk.length) throw new Error('Invalid chunk data');

        const bytesLeftInCurrentChunk = currentChunk.length - offset;

        // Determine how much can be copied from the current chunk without exceeding the desired length
        const bytesToCopyFromCurrentChunk = Math.min(bytesLeftInCurrentChunk, length - bytesCopied);

        // Copy data from the current chunk to the result buffer
        currentChunk.copy(result, bytesCopied, offset, offset + bytesToCopyFromCurrentChunk);

        // Update our counters and indexes accordingly
        bytesCopied += bytesToCopyFromCurrentChunk;
        offset = 0;  // we assume data from each chunk is continuous
        currentChunkIndex++;
    }

    return result;
}
```

Please note that JavaScript is single-threaded and doesn't support truly concurrent operations, so the `Atomics` API can't be used here. If you need to handle concurrency in NodeJS environment, consider using Workers or Cluster module instead of `Atomics`.
