["Here is an example of how you can implement such a module in JavaScript. It uses modern ES6 syntax with Promises for asynchronous loading and error handling. The module includes methods for appending new scripts to the document, logging errors and warnings, checking whether or not the script was loaded before and if it's compatible with the current environment.

```js
class DynamicScriptLoader {
    constructor() {
        this.loadedFiles = {};
        this.errorLogger = console; // Assuming console for now, but could be any logger.
    }

    load(filePath) {
        if (this.isAlreadyLoaded(filePath)) {
            return Promise.resolve(); // No need to load again, resolve immediately
        }

        this.loadedFiles[filePath] = true;

        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            const scriptElement = document.createElement('script');
            scriptElement.src = filePath;

            scriptElement.onload = () => resolve(); // Resolve the promise on load success

            scriptElement.onerror = (e) => { 
                this.logError(`Failed to load ${filePath}`, e); 
                reject(e); // Reject the promise if error during loading
            };

            document.body.appendChild(scriptElement);
        });
    }

    isAlreadyLoaded(filePath) {
        return this.loadedFiles[filePath];
    }

    logError(message, error) {
        this.errorLogger.error(`DynamicScriptLoader: ${message}`, error);
    }

    // Example method to add compatibility checks for different environments or versions of JavaScript
    isCompatibleWithEnvironment() {
        return true; // Always returning true, assuming all scripts are compatible with the environment
    }
}
```
In this example, each file path is loaded only once. If a script fails to load (due to network error, corrupted file, or incompatible with current environment), it rejects the promise and logs an error. You can append new scripts without changing the core implementation of the module. This module is flexible enough for future additions of files. 

Note: In real-world applications, you would want to implement a more robust logging system than just console.log, and handle different types of errors appropriately. Also consider using a library like jQuery or require.js that handles these details automatically. However, the above implementation should serve as a good starting point for most basic use cases.
"]

['Here is a Java code block that implements a custom data structure using `java.util.HashMap` to manage directory hierarchies and uses `java.io.File` for handling directories:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class DirectoryCounter {
    private HashMap<String, Integer> hierarchy;

    public DirectoryCounter() {
        this.hierarchory = new HashMap<>();
    }

    public int countDirectories(List<String> paths) {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        try {
            for (String path : paths) {
                File file = new File(path);

                if (file.exists() && file.isDirectory()) {
                    countDirectoryRecursive(file);
                } else {
                    System.out.println("Invalid or non-existent directory: " + path);
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred while processing file paths: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

            System.out.println("Processing time: " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
        }

        return this.hierarchy.size();
    }

    private void countDirectoryRecursive(File directory) {
        String absolutePath = directory.getAbsolutePath();

        if (!this.hierarchy.containsKey(absolutePath)) {
            this.hierarchy.put(absolutePath, 1);

            File[] subDirectories = directory.listFiles(File::isDirectory);

            for (File subDir : subDirectories) {
                countDirectoryRecursive(subDir);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This `DirectoryCounter` class manages the hierarchical structure of directories with a HashMap. It recursively traverses each directory and its subdirectories, adding them to the map if they're not already there. The class handles exceptions by printing error messages and always logs the time taken for processing. Note that it does not account for multiple threads accessing the same hierarchy at the same time (which would require synchronization), but this is a simplified example.
