['Here is a C# implementation of the `RandomValueGenerator` class. This code uses System's Random class and locks to ensure thread safety.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;

public class RandomValueGenerator
{
    private double _min, _max;
    private bool _isInclusive;
    private readonly Random _random = new Random();
    private readonly object _lockObject = new object();

    public RandomValueGenerator(double min, double max, bool isInclusive)
    {
        if (min > max) throw new ArgumentException("Minimum value cannot be greater than maximum");

        _min = min;
        _max = max;
        _isInclusive = isInclusive;
    }

    public double GenerateDouble()
    {
        lock (_lockObject)  // Ensure thread safety
        {
            if (_isInclusive) return _random.NextDouble() * (_max - _min) + _min;

            return _random.NextDouble() * (_max - _min - 1) + _min + 1;
        }
    }

    public int GenerateInt()
    {
        lock (_lockObject)  // Ensure thread safety
        {
            if (_isInclusive) return _random.Next((int)_min, (int)_max + 1);

            return _random.Next((int)_min + 1, (int)_max);
        }
    }

    public override string ToString()
    {
        lock (_lockObject)  // Ensure thread safety
        {
            return $"Range: [{(_isInclusive ? "inclusive" : "exclusive")}] ({_min}, {_max})";
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a `RandomValueGenerator` class which takes min, max values and an indicator for inclusive or exclusive generation. The `GenerateDouble` method generates a random double value in the range, taking into account whether it's inclusive or not, while `GenerateInt` generates an integer within this range according to the same principle.

When the object is converted to string using `ToString`, we display whether our generation is inclusive or exclusive and the min/max values of the current range. The whole class is thread-safe because it's locked when modifying its state. 

The Random object doesn't need to be locked as it is thread-safe itself but locking ensures consistent result during multiple threads operation on the same object.
']

