['Here is a simple example of how you might implement this using libyaml library for parsing YAML files in C. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <yaml.h>

int load_resource_bundle(const char *filepath, void **bundle) {
    FILE* fp = NULL;
    yaml_parser_t parser;
    yaml_event_t event;

    if ((fp = fopen(filepath, "r")) == NULL) {
        perror("fopen");
        return -1; /* Return -1 for file open error */
    }

    if (!yaml_parser_initialize(&parser)) {
        fclose(fp);
        return -2; /* Return -2 for YAML parser initialization error */
    }

    yaml_parser_set_input_file(&parser, fp);

    int items = 0;
    while (1) {
        if (!yaml_parser_parse(&parser, &event)) {
            const char* err = parser.error; /* Get the last error */
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to parse YAML: %s\n", err);
            yaml_parser_delete(&parser);
            fclose(fp);
            return -3; /* Return -3 for YAML parsing error */
        }

        if (event.type == YAML_STREAM_END_EVENT) {
            break;
        }

        /* Only count mappings as items */
        if (event.type == YAML_MAPPING_START_EVENT) {
            items++;
        }
    }

    yaml_parser_delete(&parser);
    fclose(fp);

    return items; /* Return the number of items in bundle */
}
```
This function opens a YAML file, parses it event by event using libyaml library, and counts mapping events which represent each item. It returns the total count of mappings found. Note that this is a simple example that doesn't handle all possible cases (for instance, it only treats items as YAML mappings).

For handling other formats such as JSON or TOML, you can use additional libraries like jansson for JSON and libtoml for TOML. Also, please ensure to check return values of functions for error checking. 

Remember that working with dynamic memory can be tricky in C - always remember to free any allocated resources once they are no longer needed. In this case, after we're done using the bundle, we should free it and any other dynamically allocated resources used by load_resource_bundle function. If your application allows for bundles of multiple types (YAML/JSON/TOML) you might want to consider having separate functions for each type of bundle.
