    private String transactionId;
    private Double amount;
    private Long timestamp;
    private String currency;
    private String status;

    // Getters and Setters...
}
```

And the `JsonObjectPooledObjectFactory` as:

```java
import org.apache.commons.pool2.BasePooledObjectFactory;
import org.apache.commons.pool2.PooledObject;
import org.apache.commons.pool2.impl.DefaultPooledObject;

public class JsonObjectPooledObjectFactory extends BasePooledObjectFactory<JsonObject> {
    @Override
    public JsonObject create() throws Exception {
        return new JsonObject();
    }

    @Override
    public PooledObject<JsonObject> wrap(JsonObject obj) {
        return new DefaultPooledObject<>(obj);
    }
}
```

This is a basic implementation and should be modified to suit specific needs. For example, the use of `ConcurrentHashMap` for caching may not always be ideal depending on requirements and performance considerations. Similarly, Apache Commons Pool2 may not be optimal in some scenarios due to additional overhead, alternatives like direct object creation or other pooling libraries might be more suitable.
']

['Here is a simple example of how you could implement this semaphore management library in C. This code does not include all the features mentioned but it gives an idea of what such a library might look like. 

Please note that developing such libraries requires a good understanding of concurrency, synchronization primitives (semaphores), and error handling. The below code is a very basic and simplified version of semaphore management.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <unistd.h>

typedef struct Semaphore {
    sem_t *mutex;
} Semaphore;

Semaphore* createSemaphore() {
    Semaphore *sem = (Semaphore*) malloc(sizeof(Semaphore));
    if (sem == NULL) return NULL;

    sem->mutex = (sem_t*) malloc(sizeof(sem_t));
    if (sem_init(sem->mutex, 0, 1) != 0) {
        free(sem);
        return NULL;
    }

    return sem;
}

void destroySemaphore(Semaphore *sem) {
    if (sem == NULL) return;

    sem_destroy(sem->mutex);
    free(sem->mutex);
    free(sem);
}

int acquire(Semaphore *sem) {
    if (sem == NULL) return -1;

    return sem_wait(sem->mutex);
}

int release(Semaphore *sem) {
    if (sem == NULL) return -1;

    return sem_post(sem->mutex);
}
```

This is a simple implementation of a counting semaphore with binary value. To make it scalable, you would need to handle thread safety and resource management effectively. You could add dynamic allocation/deallocation of semaphores based on demand or use an existing library for that purpose. For logging, you could integrate a suitable logging framework like log4c (or similar). 

Please note this code does not include many features mentioned in the task description, such as handling external data source failures, network latency, varying system resource limits and integrating with other components of a larger system. These would require a more complex and sophisticated implementation to fully address these aspects of software development.
