["Implementing this task in C is a significant undertaking given the requirements for efficiency, thread safety, compression capabilities, logging mechanisms, and future scalability. Here's a very basic example that can be adapted as needed:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <assert.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <sys/sysinfo.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int RNTI;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
    int CRCFLAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

// Use a mutex to ensure thread safety
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int nextRNTI = 0;

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *generateStructures(long long int size) {
    struct sysinfo si;
    if (sysinfo(&si)) return NULL;

    double load = ((double)(si.loads[0] + si.loads[1] + si.loads[2])) / 65536.0;
    long long int availableMemory = si.freeram * load * 0.8; // Use only up to 80% of free memory based on current system load

    size = (size > availableMemory) ? availableMemory : size; // Don't exceed the available memory

    srand(time(NULL));
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *list = malloc(sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T) * size);

    for (long long int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Lock before modifying shared state

        list[i].RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG = rand();
        list[i].RNTI = nextRNTI++;
        list[i].CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG = rand();
        list[i].CRCFLAG = rand() % 2; // Randomly set CRCFLAG to either 0 or 1

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock after modifying shared state
    }

    return list;
}
```
The `generateStructures` function first calculates the available memory based on the system's current load. It then creates an array of structures with a size not exceeding this value. For each structure, it sets random values for all its fields. The mutex ensures that only one thread can modify shared state at any time to prevent race conditions.

Please note: This is just a basic example and does not include the detailed requirements such as compression mechanism, logging mechanisms or scalability considerations. To achieve these features efficiently requires more advanced coding and design skills. Consider using existing libraries for memory management, multi-threading synchronization and logging. You may also need to consider how to handle large amounts of data when system resources are limited as this can involve complex techniques such as streaming, chunking or on-disk storage depending on the use case.
