['Below is a basic implementation of your request written in C. This code reads keys from an external file and generates random offset values for each record. It dynamically allocates memory as necessary, ensuring efficient use of resources. For I/O errors, it checks the return value of `fscanf()` and returns with an error message if reading fails:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int key;
} record;

void process_data(const char* filename, long offset_range) {
    // Allocate initial memory for arrays and records
    long *offsets = malloc(sizeof(*offsets));
    int *keys = malloc(sizeof(*keys)), size = 0;
    record *records = NULL;

    if (offsets == NULL || keys == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    FILE* file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening the file.\n");
        free(offsets);
        free(keys);
        return;
    }

    // Read keys from file
    while (fscanf(file, "%d", &keys[size]) == 1) {
        size++;
        int *temp = realloc(keys, sizeof(*keys) * (size + 1));

        if (temp == NULL) {
            printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
            free(offsets);
            free(keys);
            fclose(file);
            return;
        }

        keys = temp;
    }

    // Generate offsets and records
    srand(time(NULL));
    records = malloc(sizeof(*records) * size);

    if (records == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        free(offsets);
        free(keys);
        fclose(file);
        return;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        offsets[i] = rand() % offset_range;
        records[i].key = keys[i];
    }

    // Handle the processed data and then free the memory
    /* Processing code here */

    fclose(file);
    free(offsets);
    free(keys);
    free(records);
}
``` 
Note that this is a very basic implementation, and it has limitations. For example, it reads all keys into memory at once, which might be inefficient for large files. Future improvements could include reading the file in chunks or using a buffered reader to handle larger datasets more efficiently. Also, the code doesn't account for system responsiveness, you may need to add threads and proper error handling for real-time processing. For scalability, data structures should be optimized based on the nature of data like if it is sorted, use binary search tree or hash map etc., and algorithms should also be efficient considering time complexity, so that memory usage and execution speed can be balanced.
