Please note that this example is oversimplified for the purpose of demonstration. In real-world applications, you would need to expand upon it significantly:

```csharp
public class ConfigurationService
{
    private ConcurrentDictionary<string, RateLimit> rateLimits = new();

    // ... other service dependencies ...

    public async Task<HttpResponseMessage> ProcessPostRequest(
        string sessionId, 
        string moduleIdentifier, 
        Dictionary<string, object> configurationUpdates)
    {
        if (await rateLimits.GetOrAdd(sessionId, _ => new RateLimit()).Acquire())
            return RequestEntityTooManyRequests();

        // validate session and module identifiers
        // ...

        using var scope = serviceProvider.CreateScope();
        var moduleService = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IModuleService>();

        try
        {
            await moduleService.BeginTransactionAsync(IsolationLevel.Serializable);

            foreach (var update in configurationUpdates)
            {
                // versioning, checksum validation, caching, audit logging
                var oldConfig = await distributedCache.GetOrCreateAsync(update.Key, entry => moduleService.FetchConfigurationAsync(update.Key));

                if (!ChecksumValidator.Validate(oldConfig, update.Value))
                    return BadRequest(); // or other appropriate error response

                await moduleService.UpdateConfigurationAsync(update.Key, oldConfig, update.Value);
            }

            await moduleService.CommitTransactionAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            await moduleService.RollbackTransactionAsync();

            // log error and return appropriate response for network failures etc
            Logger.LogError(ex, "An error occurred while processing the configuration updates.");
            return InternalServerError();
        }

        return Ok(); // or some other appropriate success response
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a token bucket algorithm to limit requests to 100 per minute. Each session is represented by an entry in the rateLimits dictionary, with tokens added at a fixed rate of 60/100 = 0.6 tokens/sec. When a request comes in, it tries to acquire a token from that session's bucket. If there are no available tokens, it means too many requests have been made within the last minute and returns an HTTP 429 response (Too Many Requests).

The moduleService is used to handle all database interactions and transactions while applying configuration updates. BeginTransactionAsync begins a new transaction with isolation level Serializable to ensure that concurrent transactions are processed one at a time, maintaining consistency across modules.

FetchConfigurationAsync, UpdateConfigurationAsync and CommitTransactionAsync are just placeholders for your actual implementation of these methods. In a real-world application, they would interact with the database or some other configuration store, possibly using entity framework or Dapper.

For performance monitoring, you might set up Application Insights in combination with Azure Monitor to get alerts if certain thresholds are exceeded for specific operations. This is beyond the scope of this simple code sample and would require a more detailed setup. 

Please replace `...` parts with actual implementation based on your requirements. Make sure to handle possible null exceptions that can occur due to invalid inputs or failed database interactions.
