["Here is a skeleton for such a system in JavaScript using Promises, fetch API, and ES6 Map object. This code does not include the logic for handling external data sources or caching strategies mentioned as it would need much more details and can be quite large. 

Please note that you might need to adjust this solution based on your actual needs such as loading strategy (lazy vs eager), cache eviction policy, network error handling etc. This is a simplified version of what you are asking for:

```javascript
class ModuleLoader {
    constructor(cacheSize = 100) {
        this.cache = new Map();
        this.maxCacheSize = cacheSize;
    }

    loadModule(moduleMeta, retries = 3) {
        return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
            if (this.cache.has(moduleMeta.name)) {
                const cachedModule = this.cache.get(moduleMeta.name);

                // check for updates
                if (cachedModule.version < moduleMeta.version) {
                    fetch(`${moduleMeta.src}/meta.json?v=${Date.now()}`)
                        .then(response => response.json())
                        .then(remoteModule => {
                            if (remoteModule.version > cachedModule.version) {
                                this.cache.set(moduleMeta.name, remoteModule);
                                resolve(remoteModule);
                            } else {
                                resolve(cachedModule);
                            }
                        })
                        .catch(err => reject(err));
                } else {
                    resolve(cachedModule);
                }
            } else {
                fetch(`${moduleMeta.src}/module.js?v=${Date.now()}`)
                    .then(response => response.text())
                    .then(data => {
                        const module = {...moduleMeta, data};
                        this.cache.set(moduleMeta.name, module);
                        resolve(module);

                        if (this.cache.size > this.maxCacheSize) {
                            // clear half of the cache
                            Array.from(this.cache.keys())
                                .slice(0, this.maxCacheSize / 2)
                                .forEach(key => this.cache.delete(key));
                        }
                    })
                    .catch(err => {
                        if (retries > 0) {
                            // exponential backoff
                            setTimeout(() => this.loadModule(moduleMeta, retries - 1), Math.pow(2, 4 - retries) * 1000);
                        } else {
                            reject(err);
                        }
                    });
            }
        });
    }

    loadModules(modulesMeta) {
        return Promise.all(modulesMeta.map(module => this.loadModule(module)));
    }
}
```

To use the `ModuleLoader`:

```javascript
let loader = new ModuleLoader();
loader.loadModules([{name: 'module1', version: '1.0', src: './src/module1'}, {name: 'module2', version: '1.1', src: './src/module2'}])
    .then(modules => console.log('Loaded modules: ', modules))
    .catch(err => console.error('Failed to load modules: ', err));
``` 

The code given above uses the fetch API for asynchronous module loading, Promise object for handling asynchronous operations and ES6 Map object for caching modules. It also implements exponential backoff retry mechanism for failed network requests and cache eviction strategy based on a fixed size limit. 

Remember that this is just an example and may need to be adjusted according to your requirements.
"]

