["Here's a JavaScript implementation for this task that takes advantage of typed arrays to minimize memory usage:

```javascript
function copyData(chunks, byteCount, offset = 0) {
  if (byteCount <= 0 || chunks.length === 0 || offset < 0 || offset >= chunks[0].byteLength) {
    return new Uint8Array();
  }

  let copiedBytes = 0;
  let result = [];

  for (let chunk of chunks) {
    if (!(chunk instanceof Uint8Array)) {
      throw new TypeError('All elements in the `chunks` array must be instances of Uint8Array');
    }

    const availableBytesInChunk = chunk.byteLength - offset;
    let bytesToCopy = Math.min(availableBytesInChunk, byteCount - copiedBytes);

    if (bytesToCopy <= 0) { 
      break; 
    }

    result.push(chunk.subarray(offset, offset + bytesToCopy));
    copiedBytes += bytesToCopy;
    offset = 0; // Reset the offset for each chunk after the first one
  }

  return new Uint8Array(result);
}
```

This function starts copying from a specified offset in the first chunk and continues to copy data from subsequent chunks until either the requested byte count is reached or all chunks have been fully traversed. If the total available data across all chunks is less than the requested byte count, then only that amount of data will be returned without altering the original data chunks. 

The function also gracefully handles edge cases like when the starting offset is larger than the size of the first chunk or if no data needs to be copied (for example, when `byteCount` is zero or negative). In such cases, it returns an empty buffer. The type check for `chunks` array ensures that all elements in the array are instances of Uint8Array before proceeding with the copying process.
"]

