["Here is a basic solution using `async`/`await` syntax in JavaScript. This code provides a starting point but needs to be expanded to include more advanced features mentioned above. Also note that some of these features can't be tested here, such as rate limiting or dynamic configuration adjustments based on runtime metrics.

```javascript
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

class AsyncAggregator {
    constructor(limit = 10, maxRetries = 3, delay = 500) {
        this.concurrencyLimit = limit;
        this.maxRetries = maxRetries;
        this.delay = delay;
        this.semaphore = new Semaphore(this.concurrencyLimit);
    }

    async reduceAsync(array, initialValue, callback) {
        let result = initialValue;
        for (const value of array) {
            await this.semaphore.enter();
            try {
                const partialResult = await this._retryOperation(() => callback(result, value));
                result = partialResult;
            } catch (e) {
                console.error('Error occurred: ', e);
            } finally {
                this.semaphore.leave();
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    async _retryOperation(operation) {
        let attempts = 0;
        while (attempts < this.maxRetries) {
            try {
                const result = await operation();
                return result;
            } catch (e) {
                attempts++;
                console.warn('Attempt failed, retrying...');
                if (attempts === this.maxRetries) throw e;
                await sleep(this.delay * 2 ** attempts); // Exponential backoff
                this.delay *= 2 ** attempts;
            }
        }
    }
}

class Semaphore {
    constructor(limit) {
        this.limit = limit;
        this.counter = 0;
        this.waitingQueue = [];
    }

    async enter() {
        if (this.counter >= this.limit) {
            await new Promise((resolve) => this.waitingQueue.push(resolve));
        } else {
            this.counter++;
        }
    }

    leave() {
        if (!this.waitingQueue.length) {
            this.counter--;
            return;
        } 

        const resolve = this.waitingQueue.shift();
        resolve();
    }
}
```
This code creates a semaphore for concurrency management and implements an exponential backoff retry mechanism. It uses promises to allow the scheduler control flow when all resources are occupied, which can be helpful in high load conditions. This is a basic implementation and lacks the advanced features mentioned in the problem description. For example, it doesn't include rate limiting or dynamic configuration adjustments based on runtime metrics.
"]

