['This problem can be solved using several libraries in C such as jansson, cJSON etc. For this solution, we are going to use jansson which is a C library for encoding, decoding and manipulating JSON data. 

Here's a basic implementation. Note that you need to include the json-c library in your project:
```c
#include <jansson.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    char method[5];
    char path[2048];
    char auth_type[10];
} Endpoint;

bool validateConfig(char *configFilePath) {
    json_t *root, *endpoints;
    json_error_t error;

    root = json_load_file(configFilePath, 0, &error);
    if(!root) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error loading config file: on line %d: %s\n", error.line, error.text);
        return false;
    }

    endpoints = json_object_get(root, "endpoints"); 
    if(!json_is_array(endpoints)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Config does not contain a list of endpoints\n");
        json_decref(root);
        return false;
    }

    size_t i, size = json_array_size(endpoints);
    for(i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        Endpoint endpoint;
        json_t *data, *auth_token;

        data = json_array_get(endpoints, i); 
        if(!json_is_object(data)) continue; 

        json_unpack(data, "{s:s, s:s, s?s}", "method", endpoint.method, "path", endpoint.path, "auth_type", endpoint.auth_type);

        if(!strcmp(endpoint.method, "GET") && !strcmp(endpoint.method, "POST") && 
           !strcmp(endpoint.method, "PUT") && !strcmp(endpoint.method, "DELETE")) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Invalid method\n");
            json_decref(root);
            return false;
        }

        if(!strcmp(endpoint.auth_type, "basic") && !strcmp(endpoint.auth_type, "bearer")) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Invalid auth_type\n");
            json_decref(root);
            return false;
        } else if(!strcmp(endpoint.auth_type, "bearer") && !json_unpack(data, "{s:o}", "auth_token", &auth_token)) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Bearer token is required\n");
            json_decref(root);
            return false;
        }
    } 

    json_decref(root); 
    return true;
}
```
In this code:
1. We first load the JSON file.
2. Then we check if the loaded data is a list of endpoints.
3. For each endpoint, we unpack its fields and validate them according to the given rules.
4. If any validation fails, an error message is printed and `false` is returned.
5. Finally, we clean up our memory and return `true` if no issues were found. 

Please note that this code has room for improvement and additional edge cases should be considered based on the specific requirements of your project (like authentication token validation). Also remember to link against json-c library when compiling with gcc or similar compiler.
']

