['The given task is quite complex and requires a good understanding of audio processing libraries like libsox (or similar) and multithreading in C. Here's an example of how this could look like.

This code snippet assumes that you have FFMPEG library installed on your system, which supports conversion from any format to MP3. Also, it does not contain error handling for all possible scenarios, but gives you a basic idea about the implementation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#include <pthread.h>

#define MAX_THREADS 10   // Maximum number of threads to be created

void* convertToMp3(void *file) {
    char command[500];
    sprintf(command, "ffmpeg -i %s %s.mp3", (char*) file, (char*) file);
    system(command);
    pthread_exit(NULL);
}

int processAudioFiles(const char *path, const char *baseName, const char *extension) {
    DIR *dir;
    struct dirent *ent;
    pthread_t threads[MAX_THREADS];
    int threadCount = 0, i = 0;

    if ((dir = opendir (path)) != NULL) {
        while ((ent = readdir(dir)) != NULL) {
            char filePath[256], baseNameLength = strlen(baseName);
            snprintf(filePath, sizeof(filePath), "%s/%s", path, ent->d_name);

            if (strncmp(ent->d_name, baseName, baseNameLength) == 0 && strcmp(ent->d_name + baseNameLength, extension) == 0) {
                // Start new thread for each file
                pthread_create(&threads[i++], NULL, convertToMp3, (void*)filePath);
                if (++threadCount == MAX_THREADS) {
                    // Wait for all threads to finish before creating more
                    for(int j = 0; j < threadCount; ++j) {
                        pthread_join(threads[j], NULL);
                    }
                    threadCount = 0;
                }
            }
        }
        closedir (dir);
    } else {
        perror ("Couldn't open the directory");
        return 1;
    }

    // Wait for any remaining threads to finish
    for(int j = 0; j < threadCount; ++j) {
        pthread_join(threads[j], NULL);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This function uses a directory path, base file name and output extension to locate files that need to be converted. It starts a new thread for each file that needs conversion (up to MAX_THREADS). Once the limit is reached or if there are no more files left to process, it waits for all currently running threads to finish before starting new ones again.

Please note this is just an example and doesn't cover all your requirements like error handling, resource management etc.. This code also doesn't do any logging. For a real-world application you would need more robust solution with better error handling and logging mechanisms. You will have to handle the dependencies (database queries for metadata retrieval and file storage services) in calling function based on this.
