typedef struct TempSensor {
    float timestamp;
    double location[2];  // [latitude, longitude]
    double temperature;
} TempSensor;

TempSensor* read_sensor_data(int *n) {
    scanf("%d", n);

    TempSensor* data = (TempSensor*) malloc((*n) * sizeof(TempSensor));
    if (data == NULL) exit(1);  // handle memory allocation failure

    for (int i = 0; i < *n; ++i) {
        scanf("%f %lf %lf %lf %lf", &data[i].timestamp, &data[i].location[0],
                &data[i].location[1], &data[i].temperature);
    }

    return data;
}
```
In above code:
- read_sensor_data function reads the number of sensor readings from standard input, dynamically allocates an array of TempSensor structures and fills it with data.
- The 'TempSensor' structure is defined to hold timestamp (float), a pair of coordinates for location ([latitude, longitude] - as double) and temperature value (as double). 
- We use scanf function in C standard library to read data from standard input, where we first provide the number of sensor readings. Then for each reading we provide timestamp, latitude, longitude, and temperature.
- If malloc fails to allocate memory, it'll exit the program with status 1 (error code). It should be noted that in a real-world scenario this would need more robust error handling.
- The TempSensor* is returned as a pointer which can be used by caller to access the read data and n (number of readings) can be passed as a pointer so that it's updated with number of sensor readings read from standard input. It allows for a single pass over stdin and avoids unnecessary memory allocations when dealing with large amounts of data.
']

['Here's how you could implement such a Java function using a `HashMap` to store the counts for each book. This function uses regular expressions to find all words in the text, then checks whether each word is a part of Old Testament Books or not. The HashMap ensures that we don't need to iterate over the whole String multiple times:
```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.regex.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class Main {
    public static final Pattern WORD_PATTERN = Pattern.compile("\\b[a-zA-Z]+\\b");  // regex to find words

    public Map<String, AtomicInteger> countBooks(BibleBookChecker bibleBookChecker, String text) {
        if (bibleBookChecker == null || text == null) return new HashMap<>();

        Matcher matcher = WORD_PATTERN.matcher(text);
        Map<String, AtomicInteger> bookCounts = new HashMap<>();

        while (matcher.find()) {
            String word = text.substring(matcher.start(), matcher.end());  // get the found word from text
            try {
                if (bibleBookChecker.isOldTestamentBook(word)) {  // check if it's an Old Testament book
                    bookCounts.computeIfAbsent(word, k -> new AtomicInteger()).incrementAndGet();  // increment the count
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {  // handle any errors in BibleBookChecker
                System.err.println("Error occurred while checking " + word + ": " + e);
            }
        }

        return bookCounts;
    }
}
```
