for (Entity entity : entityList) {
    if (entity.isActive()) {
        // ...
    }
}
```
```

response:
['Here's a Java code that accomplishes the task:

```java
import java.util.*;

class Entity {
    String name;
    String location;
    boolean status; // true if active, false if inactive
    String type; 

    public Entity(String name, String location, boolean status, String type) {
        this.name = name;
        this.location = location;
        this.status = status;
        this.type = type;
    }

    // Library function to fetch the entity type
    public String getType() {
        return this.type;
    }

    // Library function to check if an entity is active
    public boolean isActive() {
        return status;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static Map<String, List<String>> categorizeEntities(List<Entity> entities) {
        Map<String, List<String>> result = new HashMap<>();

        for (Entity entity : entities) {
            if (entity != null && entity.isActive()) { // Null check and active status check 
                String type = entity.getType();

                if (!result.containsKey(type)) {
                    result.put(type, new ArrayList<>());
                }

                result.get(type).add(entity.name);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
       List<Entity> entities = Arrays.asList(
           new Entity("Entity 1", "Location 1", true, "Type A"),
           new Entity("Entity 2", "Location 2", false, "Type B"), // inactive entity
           new Entity("Entity 3", "Location 3", true, "Type A")
       );

       Map<String, List<String>> result = categorizeEntities(entities);

       for (Map.Entry<String, List<String>> entry : result.entrySet()) {
           System.out.println("Type: " + entry.getKey() + ", Entities: " + entry.getValue());
       }
    }
}
```
This code creates a map that holds entity types as keys and lists of active entities' names as values. It utilizes null checks for each entity in the list to avoid `NullPointerException`, and only processes active entities. To handle large datasets efficiently without compromising performance, it uses an efficient data structure (HashMap) for insertions and lookups, which gives constant time complexity O(1). The trade-off here is readability versus brevity, and this code is notably longer than its more compact but less clear counterpart.
']

