NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* readIntegers(int *size) {
    int capacity = 1024;  // Initial size of the array.
    *size = 0;

    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *array = malloc(capacity * sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
    if (array == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    int value;
    while (scanf("%d", &value) == 1) {  // Keep reading until EOF.
        if (*size >= capacity) {  // If array is full, reallocate with double the size.
            capacity *= 2;
            NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* temp = realloc(array, capacity * sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
            if (temp == NULL) {
                printf("Memory reallocation failed.\n");
                free(array);  // Free the original array.
                return NULL;
            }
            array = temp;
        }

        array[*size].RNTI = value;  // Populate the struct with the read value.
        (*size)++;
    }

    if (ferror(stdin)) {
        printf("Error reading from stdin.\n");
        free(array);
        return NULL;
    }

    return array;  // Return dynamically allocated array and its size.
}
```

The function `readIntegers` reads integers from the standard input, stores them in a dynamically allocated array, checks for memory allocation errors, handles EOF properly, and returns an error message if there is any issue with reading or allocating memory. 

For the second task (appending a slash to a string if it does not exceed a size limit), here's how you might write this in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char* appendSlash(const char *input, int limit) {
    if (input == NULL) return NULL;  // Null pointer check.

    size_t len = strlen(input);
    if (len > limit) return NULL;  // Size check.

    char* result = malloc((len + 2) * sizeof(char));  // Allocate memory for the new string, including space for '/' and '\0'.
    if (result == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    strcpy(result, input);  // Copy the original string.
    result[len] = '/';  // Append '/' to the end of the new string.
    result[len + 1] = '\0';  // Add null-terminator at the end.

    return result;  // Return the new string.
}
```
This function checks if the input is not NULL and within the size limit, allocates memory for a new string with space for '/' and '\0', copies the original string to this new location, appends '/', and returns the new string. If there is an issue with allocation or size check, it returns NULL.
