interface ValidationRule<T> {
    boolean validate(T device);
    String suggestion();
}

public class ConfigurationValidationFactory {
    private static final Map<String, Class<? extends ValidationRule>> rules = new HashMap<>();

    public static void registerDeviceTypeRule(String deviceType, Class<? extends ValidationRule> rule) {
        rules.put(deviceType, rule);
    }

    public static ValidationRule createValidationRuleForDevice(Device device) throws Exception {
        String deviceType = device.getClass().getName();
        if (rules.containsKey(deviceType)) {
            return rules.get(deviceType).newInstance();
        } else {
            throw new Exception("No validation rule found for this type of device");
        }
    }
}
```
In real-world use case, you'd register your ValidationRule implementations in a central configuration service or startup code. This factory pattern will allow developers to add new types of devices without modifying the existing codebase. Each device type would have its own `ValidationRule` implementation that is responsible for validating that device type correctly.

