['This task involves a complex system design which may involve multiple components. However, implementing everything from scratch is beyond reasonable here due to its complexity and size. Below is an outline for a basic implementation of some of these requirements using pthreads (POSIX threads) for concurrency control, C99 features for dynamic memory management, and callback mechanism.

This does not include all the details you mentioned due to their complexity and size but it gives you a good idea how to start implementing this kind of system:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define MAX_LIST_SIZE 10000
#define CHUNK_SIZE 512

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    int RNTI;
    int CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *data;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

typedef void (*callback)(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *);

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
Node *listHead = NULL;
int listSize = 0;

callback onDataChangedCallback = NULL;

void registerOnDataChanged(callback func) {
    onDataChangedCallback = func;
}

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* createStructure() {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *data = (NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T*)malloc(sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
    return data;
}

void addData(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (listSize >= MAX_LIST_SIZE) {
        // handle list full condition
        free(data);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return;
    }

    Node *node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    node->data = data;
    node->next = NULL;

    if (listHead == NULL) {
        listHead = node;
    } else {
        Node *current = listHead;
        while (current->next != NULL) {
            current = current->next;
        }
        current->next = node;
    }

    listSize++;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This is just a start. There are many aspects to consider for this task that involve designing the memory system, implementing concurrency controls (race conditions, deadlocks), filtering structures, periodic cleanup of old structures, logging mechanism, handling out-of-memory situations and integrating with existing codebases among others.
